<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.4.3: Using Tables to Find Function Rules</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00903</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.4.3: Using Tables to Find Function Rules</md:title>
    <md:uuid>87a8a13c-1919-4c3d-a364-6da1dee211c8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>A square that has a side length of 9 cm has an area of 81 \(cm^2\). The relationship between the side length and the area of the square is a function.
    
    
    For questions a-e use the table by answering each question.
  </item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        <para>Side Length \((cm)\)</para>
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        <para>Area \((cm^2)\)</para>
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>1</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>a. _______</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>2</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>b. _______</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>4</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>c. _______</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>6</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>d. _______</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(s\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        e. _______
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="60bc1e49-59df-48af-9cf1-c6f24dbad542" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the area in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(1 cm\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The area is 1 \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(1 cm\).</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is 1. The area is \(1 cm^2\) when the side length is \(1 cm\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="d734b03b-2f68-4d9a-ac01-c45de8b9bd8e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the area in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(2 cm\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The area is 4 \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(2 cm\).</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is 4. The area is \(4 cm^2\) when the side length is \(2 cm\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="5ad92f96-7e7a-4767-824e-8d30915336d7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the area in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(4 cm\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The area is \(16 cm^2\) when the side length is \(4 cm\).</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is 16. The area is \(16 cm^2\) when the side length is \(4 cm\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="042e8ace-36ac-417f-b4a8-f3abc655352e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the area in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(6 cm\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The area is \(36 cm^2\) when the side length is \(6 cm\).</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is 36. The area is \(36 cm^2\) when the side length is \(6 cm\).</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write a rule for a function, \(A\), that gives the area of the square in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(s\) \(cm\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(A(s)= s^2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What does \(A(2)\) represent in this situation? What is its value? Write your answers, then click <emphasis>solution</emphasis> to compare your answer.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(A(2)\) is the area of the square in cm2 when the side length is 2 cm. Its value is 4.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Sketch a graph of this function.
    Use the desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course.
  </item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
